<section class="content">

<h2>Big Question</h2>

<p class="big-question">Why do we need priests? Can't we go directly to God?</p>


<p>
In 1521, a Spanish soldier named Íñigo López de Loyola was struck by a cannonball at the Battle of Pamplona. His leg shattered, he spent months in recovery. Bored and bedridden, he asked for chivalric romances to read. There were none in the castle. They brought him a life of Christ and a book of saints. He began to read, and something shifted. "What if I could do what these saints did?" he wondered. Íñigo became Ignatius, and the soldier became a priest. He once said: "Go, set the world on fire." Holy Orders is that same fire. A man is configured to Christ the Head, ordained not for himself but for the people of God. He becomes a bridge between heaven and earth.
</p>


</section>

